WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:06.040
The one question we get asked the most is
probably, “What should I invest in?”

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:12.840
And we’re tempted to share hot tips about
*#&amp;%&amp;#** and #$@!*

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:16.020
seriously, put your money there and you can’t
lose.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:19.760
But instead, we usually just say, “It depends.”

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:24.120
Investing should always be looked at through
your personal context.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:28.860
What might be a sound investment for your
bestie might be totally inappropriate for you.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:33.460
So if you’re considering dipping your toe
into investing and wondering where to start,

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:39.040
the first step is to ask yourself 5 important
questions before putting any money on the line.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:52.220
When confronted with an investment opportunity,
most people wonder how much money they’ll

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:56.900
make, when the more appropriate question is
“How Much Am I Willing to Lose?”

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:02.360
If you have a thousand dollars to invest,
how would you feel if you lost a hundred of that.

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:03.700
Two hundred?

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:04.729
Five hundred?

00:01:04.729 --> 00:01:09.950
Now, if the thought of losing money at all
makes you sweat a little, you’re not alone.

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:15.659
“Loss Aversion” is the term psychologists
use for our tendency to fear losses more than

00:01:15.659 --> 00:01:16.990
we enjoy gains.

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:22.090
It’s why many investors sold off their holdings
during the market crash of 2008.

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:26.729
They overestimated their risk tolerance when
times were good, and ended up selling at a

00:01:26.729 --> 00:01:29.340
loss when the seas got stormy.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:35.079
Knowing your personal threshold for this loss-pain
(even short-term) is a great start to knowing

00:01:35.079 --> 00:01:37.520
what kind of investment is right for you.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:43.170
The second question you should ask yourself
is what is the goal or purpose of this investment?

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:44.649
Is it for your retirement?

00:01:44.649 --> 00:01:45.909
For your child’s college?

00:01:45.909 --> 00:01:47.390
A trip around the world?

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:51.530
Each of these goals probably has a completely
different time-scale.

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:55.880
If your goal is far into the future, you could
probably handle a more aggressive long-term

00:01:55.880 --> 00:02:00.620
investment option like the stock market or
real estate and take on more risk.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:05.460
That way you can leverage the longer time-line
and put yourself in the position to receive

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:10.370
the maximum reward while also allowing yourself
space to recover from a recession.

00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:15.270
But if you are just a year or two from your
investment goal, a dip in the market might

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:19.520
mean you can’t afford to pay for that goal,
so you’re probably better off sticking with

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:23.280
something less volatile, like a CD or a bond
fund..

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:28.000
One of the habits in Stephen Covey’s classic
“7 Habits of Highly Successful People”

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.110
is: Begin with the end in mind.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:34.330
And nowhere is this more true than in investing.

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:38.390
As you consider your goals you should ultimately
make a decision on the circumstances that

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:41.040
would lead you to selling the investment.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.700
This helps you avoid selling in a panic or
hopping from one “greener pasture” to another.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:50.800
For example, you might decide that you plan
to own an investment for at least 10 years.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:54.400
Or that you’ll get out if the investment
loses 10%.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:59.140
Knowing these details in advance will help
you both pick an investment with realistic

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:03.460
goals, and help you keep your head if the
news headlines get hysterical.

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:09.310
It’s okay to allow your rules to be a little
flexible, but having no plan at all can lead

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:14.180
to your decisions being driven by emotions
like greed, fear, or panic.

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:20.040
Many brokers and investment companies are
notorious for hiding fees or making them super-complicated,

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:24.170
so it can be tricky to figure out exactly
how much an investment cost.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:28.180
Sometimes there’s a simple one-time fee
— like a stock-trading service that charges

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:29.200
per-trade.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.160
Mutual funds and index funds, on the other
hand, charge you a percentage of the money

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:39.320
you invest every year, and perhaps even an
extra sales-charge in the form of a “front-end-load”.

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:43.560
An investment advisor will typically add an
additional fee-layer for the service.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:47.540
So if you hire one, be sure they’re providing
plenty of value.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:52.640
Even investment options that appear to be
free, like CD’s and high-yield savings accounts

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:54.990
come with “opportunity costs.”

00:03:54.990 --> 00:04:00.450
They’re re-investing your money into higher-paying
investments and pocketing the difference.

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:05.140
Free investment apps like Robinhood generate
income by earning interest on whatever cash

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:08.000
you have in your account that isn’t currently
in an investment.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:13.900
To be clear, paying a price to invest isn’t
bad, or even something you can always avoid.

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:17.560
Just make sure you know what you’re paying,
so you can decide for yourself if you’re

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:18.560
getting your money’s worth.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:23.229
It would be fine to put all your money on
a single roll of the dice if you could be

00:04:23.229 --> 00:04:24.229
certain of the outcome.

00:04:24.229 --> 00:04:27.860
But in the real world, there’s never a sure-thing.

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:33.340
Spreading your assets out and avoiding over-concentration
can help ensure that your fate isn’t in

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:36.840
the hands of any single company, sector, or
industry.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:40.520
For example, let’s say you have most of
your savings in the stock of the company you

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:41.690
work for.

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:44.500
What would happen if your employer suddenly
went belly-up?

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:48.900
You’d not only be out of a job, but that
money you were counting on for retirement

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:50.410
could evaporate.

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:55.600
A financial advisor might recommend selling
off some of that stock and diversifying elsewhere,

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:58.440
like buying a home or opening up an IRA.

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:00.800
Remember that 2008 crash?

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.540
Many people had the bulk of their net worth
tied up in their homes and couldn’t afford

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:07.210
to ride out the storm.

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:08.689
Balance is key.

00:05:08.689 --> 00:05:12.840
So whenever you’re trying to decide on a
new investment, consider how it relates to

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:14.770
the rest of your assets.

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:20.500
If there were a one-size fits all that worked
for every person in every situation, it would

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:22.319
make our job a lot easier.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:26.400
But picking the right investment doesn’t
have to be as overwhelming as you might think.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:31.480
Asking yourself a few basic questions can
go a long way to simplifying which choice

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:35.490
is best for you no matter what surprises might
be around the corner.

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:37.340
And that’s our two cents!

